cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata below.
title: >-
  Degree-Independent Control Impact in the Human Structural Connectome:
  Graph-Geometric Associations and Degree-Preserving Null Arbitration
  (Paper 2, cross-species CIS series; frozen release v2.0.1)
authors:
  - family-names: Malipeddi
    given-names: Harsha Vardhan
    email: harshavardhanmalipeddi1@gmail.com
    affiliation: Independent Researcher
version: v2.0.1
date-released: "2026-09-30"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/harsha-vardhan-2006/humanbrain-hidden-chokepoint-mechanics"
abstract: >-
  A degree-independent control-impact (CIS) residual replicates across all
  801 QC-pass subjects of the AOMIC ID1000 structural connectome cohort
  (delta = 0.104, CI [0.1007, 0.1073]) and is predictable out-of-sample
  from measured network features (ridge cv-R2 = 0.574; degree-only ~ 0).
  Under 1,200 exact degree-preserving nulls with per-null full CIS and
  feature recomputation, every observed feature-residual association falls
  inside its null p95 band (pre-registered Outcome C): the proposed
  graph-geometric mechanism is not established, and the "hidden
  chokepoint" remains an operational construct only. The fly connectome's
  residual fails its degree-preserving null identically (z = 1.21,
  p = 0.109, preserved negative).
keywords:
  - structural connectome
  - network control theory
  - degree confounding
  - degree-preserving null models
  - negative result
  - human connectome
  - Drosophila connectome
